1️⃣ What is Thread Priority?

Thread priority is a hint to the JVM Thread Scheduler about which
 thread should get CPU preference when multiple threads are in runnable state.
⚠️ Priority does NOT guarantee execution order
2️⃣ Priority Range in Java

Java supports 10 priority levels:

1  → MIN_PRIORITY
5  → NORM_PRIORITY (default)
10 → MAX_PRIORITY


Constants:

Thread.MIN_PRIORITY   // 1
Thread.NORM_PRIORITY  // 5
Thread.MAX_PRIORITY   // 10
Default Priority Behavior

Main thread priority = 5

Child thread inherits parent’s priority
Setting Thread Priority
Syntax
thread.setPriority(int priority);

Example
class PriorityDemo extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getName() + " : " +
            Thread.currentThread().getPriority()
        );
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityDemo t1 = new PriorityDemo();
        PriorityDemo t2 = new PriorityDemo();

        t1.setPriority(10);
        t2.setPriority(1);

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}


📌 Higher priority thread MAY execute first, but not guaranteed
Invalid Priority → Runtime Error=>IllegalArgumentException
Thread Scheduler is JVM + OS dependent

Priority is only a suggestion

JVM may ignore priority
7️⃣ OS Dependency (INTERVIEW GOLD ⭐)
OS Type	Priority Effect
Windows	Weak priority enforcement
Linux	Time-slicing dominates
Real-time OS	Priority respected more

📌 Same program → different output on different machines
✅ CASE 1: Main thread → Child thread (default priority)
Main thread priority = 5

Child created by main

Child inherits 5

📌 Most basic inheritance

✅CASE 2: Main thread priority changed BEFORE child creation:
Main priority changed to 8

Child thread created after change

Child inherits 8

📌 Priority at creation time matters
Inheritance is from creator thread, not main.
✅ CASE 4: Parent changes its priority AFTER child creation
Code
class Case4 extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Child priority: " + getPriority());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.currentThread().setPriority(6);

        Case4 t = new Case4();   // child created here
        Thread.currentThread().setPriority(9); // change later

        t.start();
    }
}

Output
Child priority: 6

Explanation

Child is created when main priority = 6

Parent priority changed later to 9

Child does NOT change

Priority inheritance happens only once at creation

📌 No dynamic inheritance
