🔹 Hook Thread (Shutdown Hook) in Java
A Hook Thread (Shutdown Hook) is a special thread that JVM executes just before it shuts down.

📌 Used for:
Resource cleanup
Logging
Closing DB connections
Releasing files
Graceful shutdown
hook Thread
just like normal thread
just before abnormal termination hook thread is exceuted
2️⃣ When does JVM shut down?

JVM shuts down when:
System.exit() is called
Main thread finishes and no non-daemon threads exist
User presses Ctrl + C
OS shutdown / kill signal

➡️ Before JVM exits, it runs all registered hook threads

How to create a Hook Thread?:
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(Thread t);

Basic Example (MOST IMPORTANT)
class HookDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread hook = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Shutdown hook executing...");
        });

        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(hook);

        System.out.println("Main thread working...");

        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (Exception e) {}

        System.out.println("Main thread ending...");
    }
}
Output (Normal Execution)
Main thread working...
Main thread ending...
Shutdown hook executing...


📌 Hook executes after main ends but before JVM exits
